<?php
/**
 * Runtime Environment  (beta.10 C4 / F8)
 *
 * Copy this file to environment.php to change the environment. If the live
 * file is absent, QuickSite behaves as 'production' (the secure default).
 *
 * Production is also what you get from every FAILURE path: an unreadable file,
 * a syntax error, a file that is not PHP at all, a wrong shape, a missing key,
 * or any value other than the exact string 'development'. Only a well-formed
 * file saying exactly 'development' opens the gate, and a broken file is
 * reported to the PHP error log rather than taking the request down.
 *
 * Values:
 *   'production'   (default) — server-side fetches (resolvers, testApiEndpoint,
 *                  OAuth, URL asset import) MAY NOT reach loopback / private /
 *                  cloud-metadata addresses. This is what a public or shared
 *                  deployment must run as. API error responses carry no
 *                  diagnostic detail.
 *   'development'  — lifts the internal-address block so a local author can
 *                  call http://localhost:3000 or a LAN API while building.
 *                  The http/https scheme allowlist and DNS-pinning stay on.
 *                  Fatal-error responses additionally carry the error type,
 *                  message, file and line.
 *
 * SECURITY: only ever run 'development' on a machine you fully trust and that
 * is not reachable by untrusted callers. Changing this is a deliberate
 * server-side edit — it is intentionally NOT a runtime API setting, so a
 * leaked/low-trust token can never flip the environment to re-open SSRF.
 * (A future setup.bat/setup.sh step will offer to write this at install time.)
 *
 * ALSO SET display_errors = Off IN YOUR PRODUCTION php.ini.
 * On a 'production' install QuickSite turns display_errors off itself, on every
 * request, as part of installing its fatal-error handler — so an application
 * fatal cannot print the failing file's absolute path to a visitor. That only
 * covers failures from the point the engine starts. A fatal in the bootstrap
 * BEFORE it — a corrupt init.php, a broken PHP extension — runs on whatever
 * php.ini says, and there is no application code left to intercept it. Setting
 * it off at the php.ini level closes that window too, and costs nothing: pair
 * it with log_errors = On so the operator still gets the error.
 *
 * On a 'development' install error output stays on deliberately. That is the
 * point of the setting — do not run a reachable deployment that way.
 */

return [
    'environment' => 'production',
];
